1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,022
Put one grain of rice on the
first square of a chessboard.

2
00:00:04,022 --> 00:00:05,213
Two on the next.

3
00:00:05,213 --> 00:00:06,772
Then four, then eight.

4
00:00:07,173 --> 00:00:10,373
Diya heard this old story in
the kitchen, sorting rice.

5
00:00:10,373 --> 00:00:12,415
Each square doubles the one before.

6
00:00:12,865 --> 00:00:14,719
There are sixty four squares.

7
00:00:14,719 --> 00:00:16,295
How many grains in all?

8
00:00:16,645 --> 00:00:19,092
The answer is a number with twenty digits.

9
00:00:19,092 --> 00:00:21,539
Adding them one by one would take forever.

10
00:00:22,039 --> 00:00:26,973
To add a doubling list fast, we need
the sum of a geometric progression.

11
00:00:27,623 --> 00:00:28,523
Quick reminder.

12
00:00:28,508 --> 00:00:31,359
AGP multiplies by the
same number, r, each time.

13
00:00:31,359 --> 00:00:32,583
Its first term is a.

14
00:00:32,983 --> 00:00:34,511
Watch the board fill.

15
00:00:34,511 --> 00:00:36,544
After one square, one grain.

16
00:00:36,544 --> 00:00:38,369
After two squares, three.

17
00:00:38,369 --> 00:00:39,838
After three, seven.

18
00:00:39,838 --> 00:00:41,355
After four, fifteen.

19
00:00:41,755 --> 00:00:42,784
See the pattern?

20
00:00:42,784 --> 00:00:46,106
Each total is just one less
than what the next square holds.

21
00:00:46,706 --> 00:00:51,995
So after all sixty four squares, the
total is two to the power sixty four,

22
00:00:51,995 --> 00:00:52,943
minus one.

23
00:00:53,593 --> 00:00:54,952
Why does this work?

24
00:00:54,952 --> 00:00:57,057
Here is the trick for any GP.

25
00:00:57,457 --> 00:00:58,357
Step one.

26
00:00:58,259 --> 00:00:59,363
Write the sum.

27
00:00:59,363 --> 00:01:04,702
S n is a, plus a r, plus a r squared,
up to a r to the power n minus one.

28
00:01:05,452 --> 00:01:06,352
Step two.

29
00:01:06,326 --> 00:01:08,246
Multiply every term by r.

30
00:01:08,246 --> 00:01:12,917
r S n is a r, plus a r squared,
up to a r to the power n.

31
00:01:13,667 --> 00:01:14,567
Step three.

32
00:01:14,566 --> 00:01:15,621
Line them up.

33
00:01:15,621 --> 00:01:17,936
Almost every term is in both rows.

34
00:01:17,936 --> 00:01:20,340
Subtract, and they cancel in pairs.

35
00:01:21,090 --> 00:01:22,957
Only two terms survive.

36
00:01:22,957 --> 00:01:27,057
r S n minus S n is a r
to the power n, minus a.

37
00:01:27,807 --> 00:01:28,756
Step four.

38
00:01:28,756 --> 00:01:29,999
Take out S n.

39
00:01:29,999 --> 00:01:34,755
S n times r minus one is a times
r to the power n, minus one.

40
00:01:35,505 --> 00:01:36,482
Step five.

41
00:01:36,482 --> 00:01:38,337
Divide by r minus one.

42
00:01:38,337 --> 00:01:43,180
S n is a times r to the power
n minus one, over r minus one.

43
00:01:43,930 --> 00:01:45,324
Check the chessboard.

44
00:01:45,324 --> 00:01:46,992
a is one and r is two.

45
00:01:46,992 --> 00:01:50,054
We get two to the power
sixty four, minus one.

46
00:01:50,054 --> 00:01:50,954
It matches.

47
00:01:51,571 --> 00:01:52,711
Side by side.

48
00:01:52,711 --> 00:01:57,800
When r is bigger than one, use r to
the power n minus one, over r minus

49
00:01:57,800 --> 00:01:58,700
one.

50
00:01:58,339 --> 00:02:00,451
Top and bottom stay positive.

51
00:02:00,851 --> 00:02:06,067
When r is smaller than one, use one
minus r to the power n, over one minus

52
00:02:06,067 --> 00:02:06,967
r.

53
00:02:06,497 --> 00:02:08,375
Same value, just tidier.

54
00:02:08,975 --> 00:02:09,883
Your turn.

55
00:02:09,883 --> 00:02:12,876
Five, plus five, plus five, ten times.

56
00:02:12,876 --> 00:02:14,117
Here r is one.

57
00:02:14,117 --> 00:02:15,890
Can we use the formula?

58
00:02:15,890 --> 00:02:17,173
Pause and think.

59
00:02:20,373 --> 00:02:21,273
No.

60
00:02:20,844 --> 00:02:24,809
r minus one would be zero,
and we cannot divide by zero.

61
00:02:24,809 --> 00:02:29,061
When r is one, every term is
a, so the sum is n times a.

62
00:02:29,061 --> 00:02:30,158
Here, fifty.

63
00:02:30,808 --> 00:02:31,942
Remember this.

64
00:02:31,942 --> 00:02:35,841
Multiply by r, subtract, and
almost everything cancels.

65
00:02:36,541 --> 00:02:38,231
Now, one worked example.

66
00:02:38,581 --> 00:02:43,071
Find the sum of the first six terms
of two, plus six, plus eighteen, and

67
00:02:43,071 --> 00:02:43,971
so on.

68
00:02:44,192 --> 00:02:45,092
Step one.

69
00:02:45,017 --> 00:02:45,917
a is two.

70
00:02:45,903 --> 00:02:48,449
r is six over two, which is three.

71
00:02:48,449 --> 00:02:49,596
And n is six.

72
00:02:50,346 --> 00:02:51,246
Step two.

73
00:02:51,210 --> 00:02:56,108
S six is two, times three to the
power six minus one, over three minus

74
00:02:56,108 --> 00:02:57,008
one.

75
00:02:57,395 --> 00:02:58,295
Step three.

76
00:02:58,202 --> 00:03:01,457
Three to the power six is
seven hundred and twenty nine.

77
00:03:01,457 --> 00:03:04,667
So the top is two times seven
hundred and twenty eight.

78
00:03:05,417 --> 00:03:06,317
Step four.

79
00:03:06,233 --> 00:03:07,292
Divide by two.

80
00:03:07,292 --> 00:03:09,945
The sum is seven hundred and twenty eight.

81
00:03:10,695 --> 00:03:15,297
Check: two, six, eighteen, fifty
four, one hundred and sixty two, four

82
00:03:15,297 --> 00:03:16,847
hundred and eighty six.

83
00:03:16,847 --> 00:03:19,569
They add to seven
hundred and twenty eight.

84
00:03:20,219 --> 00:03:21,681
Now, the exam trap.

85
00:03:22,031 --> 00:03:26,321
In the sum formula, many students
write r to the power n minus one, like

86
00:03:26,321 --> 00:03:27,947
the formula for one term.

87
00:03:28,347 --> 00:03:30,652
But the sum uses r to the power n.

88
00:03:30,652 --> 00:03:35,235
For our six terms, three to the five
gives only two hundred and forty two.

89
00:03:35,235 --> 00:03:38,188
The right sum is seven
hundred and twenty eight.

90
00:03:38,838 --> 00:03:40,275
Where is this asked?

91
00:03:40,275 --> 00:03:41,268
In JEE Main.

92
00:03:41,268 --> 00:03:45,811
In September twenty twenty, a paper
gave sums of a few terms of a GP,

93
00:03:45,811 --> 00:03:48,897
and asked for the sum of
its first fifty terms.

94
00:03:49,497 --> 00:03:50,677
Exam level now.

95
00:03:50,677 --> 00:03:55,786
How many terms of three, plus six,
plus twelve, and so on, add up to seven

96
00:03:55,786 --> 00:03:57,425
hundred and sixty five?

97
00:03:57,425 --> 00:03:58,655
Pause and solve.

98
00:04:04,055 --> 00:04:05,496
The answer is B.

99
00:04:05,496 --> 00:04:06,580
Eight terms.

100
00:04:07,080 --> 00:04:09,467
Here a is three and r is two.

101
00:04:09,467 --> 00:04:13,716
So S n is three times, two
to the power n, minus one.

102
00:04:14,216 --> 00:04:18,691
Set it equal to seven hundred and
sixty five, and divide by three.

103
00:04:18,691 --> 00:04:23,014
Two to the power n, minus one,
is two hundred and fifty five.

104
00:04:23,514 --> 00:04:26,891
So two to the power n is
two hundred and fifty six.

105
00:04:26,891 --> 00:04:29,721
That is two to the eight, so n is eight.

106
00:04:30,321 --> 00:04:31,861
Here is your recap.

107
00:04:31,861 --> 00:04:33,444
Screenshot this card.

108
00:04:36,644 --> 00:04:38,830
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

109
00:04:39,230 --> 00:04:40,130
One.

110
00:04:39,672 --> 00:04:42,663
Why do almost all the terms
cancel when we subtract?

111
00:04:43,563 --> 00:04:44,463
Two.

112
00:04:44,041 --> 00:04:48,095
Find the sum of one, plus three,
plus nine, up to six terms.

113
00:04:48,995 --> 00:04:49,895
Three.

114
00:04:49,497 --> 00:04:51,899
What is the sum of a GP when r is one?

115
00:04:52,999 --> 00:04:55,469
Next time: two kinds of average.

116
00:04:55,469 --> 00:04:57,500
Which one is never smaller?

117
00:04:57,500 --> 00:05:01,029
The geometric mean, and
the AMGM inequality.
